          "title": "The letters in this word",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ਰੱਬ (rabb, \"God\" — note the addak doubling on ੱਬ) + ਰਾਖਾ (rākhā, \"keeper, protector\"). Read rabb rākhā."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ਰੱਬ ਰਾਖਾ means \"[may] God [be your] keeper\" — rabb (\"God\") + rākhā (\"protector,\" from rakhṇā, \"to keep, protect,\" from Sanskrit rakṣ-, \"to guard,\" the root of rākṣas and of English-borrowed rakhi). The word for God, ਰੱਬ (rabb), is itself telling: it is Arabic rabb (\"lord, master\") — the same rabb of Arabic rabb al-ʿālamīn (\"Lord of the worlds\"). So this most Punjabi of farewells fuses an Arabic name for God with a Sanskrit verb of keeping — Punjabi's two vocabularies in one blessing. Warmer still: rabb rākhā, phir milāṁge (\"God keep you, we'll meet again\")."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
